{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "Eighty per cent of chronic non-communicable disease (NCD) mortality occurs in developing countries, particularly in Africa. 1 ... For adults aged 18 - 64 years, the WHO recommends 150 min of moderate-intensity aerobic physical activity or at least 75 min of vigorous-intensity aerobic physical activity throughout the week, or an equivalent combination of moderate-intensity and vigorous-intensity physical activity; plus muscle strengthening activities involving major muscle groups done on two or more days a week. 24 However, independent of gender, ethnicity and income, more than half of all adults in Africa are inactive. 25",
      "reason": "The introduction describes the current state of knowledge on NCD burden in Africa, recommended physical activity levels and the high prevalence of inactivity, identifying important contextual uncertainties and gaps."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "No systematic review has examined the effects of behaviour change interventions on physical activity behaviour of people living with chronic NCDs in Africa; patients' experiences of these interventions; and to what extent interventions addressed patients' concerns. These are the objectives of this study.",
      "reason": "The authors explicitly state the gap in the literature and frame the objectives, explaining the importance and need for conducting this review."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "No systematic review has examined the effects of behaviour change interventions on physical activity behaviour of people living with chronic NCDs in Africa; patients' experiences of these interventions; and to what extent interventions addressed patients' concerns.",
      "reason": "The manuscript explicitly reports that no prior systematic review has examined this topic in Africa, so explaining the need relative to existing reviews is not applicable."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_16",
      "quote": "Physical activity behaviour may be influenced by motivational interviewing techniques, such as eliciting talk about change, using enthusiastic communication styles and encouraging group solutions. 41,47 Emphasizing chronic disease complications may further increase the motivation to improve physical activity. 41 Actual performance of the desired physical activity may increase physical activity through increasing self-efficacy. 41",
      "reason": "The discussion outlines plausible mechanisms (e.g. motivational interviewing, emphasis on health consequences, increased self-efficacy) by which the interventions might affect physical activity."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "No evidence in the provided text indicates that a logic model was considered or presented to show hypothesised relationships between intervention components and outcomes."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 85
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_4",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "No systematic review has examined the effects of behaviour change interventions on physical activity behaviour of people living with chronic NCDs in Africa; patients ' experiences of these interventions; and to what extent interventions addressed patients ' concerns. These are the objectives of this study.",
      "reason": "The manuscript explicitly states the review objectives (effects of behaviour-change interventions on physical activity behaviour, patients' experiences, and extent interventions addressed patients' concerns)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e2",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "Studies were selected if they involved adults ( ≥ 18 years) living in Africa with hypertension, diabetes, obesity or chronic musculoskeletal pain. There were no restrictions on study design, comparators and timing. Clinical studies (randomized controlled trials [RCTs], noncontrolled before and after [B/A] studies and mixed-methods B/ A studies) were included if they investigated the effects of physical activity interventions on physical activity behaviour.",
      "reason": "The review specifies the population (adults in Africa with specified NCDs) and the intervention (physical activity interventions) and the primary outcome (physical activity behaviour), but does not state planned comparisons in PICO format (comparators are not specified; in fact the text says there were no restrictions on comparators)."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 85
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_5",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e1",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Following PRISMA guidelines, 36 we searched MEDLINE, EMBASE, PsycINFO, CENTRAL, CINAHL, Web of Science, African Index Medicus (AIM) and AFROLIB (WHO ' s regional of fi ce database for Africa) from their inception dates up to 19 April 2017. We used a combination of Medical Subject Headings, free text terms and word variants for hypertension, diabetes, obesity and chronic musculoskeletal pain conditions, African search fi lter, 37 word variants for physical activity-related therapies and the search fi lters for three study types - clinical trials, qualitative and observational studies (supplemental fi le). ... Lung diseases were not included because it was envisaged that a signi fi cant proportion may be secondary to communicable diseases, such as tuberculosis.",
      "reason": "The manuscript specifies eligible conditions, databases searched, and eligible study types (clinical trials, qualitative, observational) and gives setting information elsewhere (tables). However it does not explicitly state follow-up requirements or a comprehensive, single list of all study characteristics used to determine eligibility (e.g. explicit follow-up length criteria are not reported), so completeness is partial."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e2",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "we searched MEDLINE, EMBASE, PsycINFO, CENTRAL, CINAHL, Web of Science, African Index Medicus (AIM) and AFROLIB ... from their inception dates up to 19 April 2017. ... We also searched grey literature as described in the protocol.",
      "reason": "The review reports the date range for searches (inception to 19 April 2017) and that grey literature was searched, but it does not state eligibility restrictions (or lack thereof) regarding language or publication/report status explicitly in the provided evidence, so the report-characteristics criterion is only partially addressed."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e3",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Reasons for exclusion: ... 2. a. Intervention: No physical activity component 2. b. Outcome evaluation: No physical activity behaviour assessment 2. c. Qualitative and quantitative observational studies that did not directly assess patients' experiences/ perspectives to physical activity interventions",
      "reason": "The flow diagram (Figure 1) explicitly lists exclusion due to 'No physical activity behaviour assessment' (i.e. outcome not measured). There is no explicit evidence in the provided text that the review separately recorded exclusions where outcomes were measured but outcome results were not reported, so the distinction is only partially documented."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e4",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_5",
      "quote": "Three-stage analyses were carried out using a mixed-studies synthesis design - the sequential explanatory synthesis.",
      "reason": "The review specifies how studies were grouped for synthesis (clinical studies summarized via narrative synthesis; qualitative studies via thematic synthesis; and a comparative analysis linking clinical and qualitative findings) and describes comparability approaches (e.g. effect sizes for RCTs and B/A studies). However, there is limited explicit linking of these groups to specific comparisons defined in the review objectives in the provided evidence, so this element is only partially satisfied."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e5",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "These conditions were selected because they are the most prevalent in Africa, 1,38 and may be the most easily modi fi ed by physical activity. Lung diseases were not included because it was envisaged that a signi fi cant proportion may be secondary to communicable diseases, such as tuberculosis.",
      "reason": "The manuscript provides explicit rationale for notable eligibility restrictions (why certain chronic conditions were selected and why lung diseases were excluded)."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 85
}

